{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.093,
            0.744,
            0.155
          ],
          "content": "**Compare** whether your observations match with your predictions. Did your right hand feel that the water in container B is cool, while your left hand felt that"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.155,
            0.589,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": "the same water is warm? What do you **infer** from these observations?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.196,
            0.579,
            0.257
          ],
          "content": "We cannot always rely upon our sense of touch to decide correctly whether a body is hot or cold."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.614,
            0.169,
            0.783,
            0.241
          ],
          "content": "Then how do we find out how hot or cold a body is?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.271,
            0.356,
            0.295
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.2 Temperature"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.305,
            0.742,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": "A reliable measure of hotness (or coldness) of a body is its **temperature**. A hotter body has a higher temperature than a colder body. The difference in temperature between the two bodies tells us how hot a body is in comparison to another body. A device that measures temperature is called a **thermometer**."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.743,
            0.234,
            0.86,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_2_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.429,
            0.745,
            0.573
          ],
          "content": "There are two kinds of thermometers that you are likely to come across—clinical thermometers and laboratory thermometers. Clinical thermometers are used to measure human body temperatures whereas laboratory thermometers are used for many other purposes. Let us now learn more about thermometers and how to use them to measure temperature."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.584,
            0.505,
            0.61
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.3 Measuring Temperature"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.617,
            0.452,
            0.638
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "7.3.1 Clinical thermometer"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.646,
            0.745,
            0.751
          ],
          "content": "You might be familiar with a thermometer, like that shown in Fig. 7.2, which is used for measuring our body temperature. It is called a clinical thermometer. Such thermometers show temperatures digitally. These are also known as digital clinical thermometers and run on batteries. These measure"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.769,
            0.759,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
